Subject: [PATCH] VideoWorker: free framesource VB pool on teardown (+ correct order) — fixes T23 rebuild leak
Target: prudynt-t @ f4b32289359d176fb0795d9f3735af0bd9d6d2fd (branch stable)

On Ingenic T23 (isp_bypass=true), an in-process video rebuild (global_restart_video,
e.g. a stream-config change) worked the first 1-2 times and then faulted the VPU
(hwicodec: vpu channel run failed / i264e_slice_write failed / ijpege_icodec_enc
failed), sometimes ending in a segfault, freezing the stream until reboot.

Root cause: the worker teardown only DISABLED the framesource
(IMP_FrameSource_DisableChn) and never destroyed it. IMP_FrameSource_DestroyChn is
only reachable via ~IMPFramesource -> destroy(), and the IMPFramesource object was
never deleted on the rebuild path (the pointer was overwritten by the next
createNew). So the video-buffer (VB) pool that IMP_FrameSource_CreateChn allocates
(nrVBs frames in rmem, tens of MB/channel) was re-allocated every rebuild and never
freed -> the rmem/VB pool was exhausted after 1-2 rebuilds. The old prudynt leaked
far less per rebuild (1 small VB vs the new auto-buffer count of 2-3 large VBs per
channel), so it stayed under the pool ceiling and appeared to work indefinitely.

Fix: in the worker teardown, tear the encoder down first (it is IMP_System_Bind-ed to
the framesource), then DISABLE and DELETE the framesource. delete runs
~IMPFramesource -> destroy() -> IMP_FrameSource_DestroyChn, which frees the VB pool,
so the next rebuild's CreateChn starts clean and rebuilds can repeat indefinitely.
The encoder path already deletes correctly; this makes the framesource path match it.

diff --git a/src/VideoWorker.cpp b/src/VideoWorker.cpp
--- a/src/VideoWorker.cpp
+++ b/src/VideoWorker.cpp
@@ -830,15 +830,23 @@
   ret = IMP_Encoder_StopRecvPic(encChn);
   LOG_DEBUG_OR_ERROR(ret, "IMP_Encoder_StopRecvPic(" << encChn << ")");

-  if (global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource) {
-    global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource->disable();
-
-    if (global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder) {
-      global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder->deinit();
-      delete global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder;
-      global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder = nullptr;
-    }
-  }
+  // T23 rebuild fix: destroy the encoder first (it is IMP_System_Bind-ed to the
+  // framesource), then DISABLE *and* DELETE the framesource. Teardown previously
+  // only DisableChn'd it, so the VB pool IMP_FrameSource_CreateChn allocates
+  // leaked every in-process rebuild and exhausted rmem after 1-2 rebuilds
+  // (hwicodec: vpu channel run failed / segfault). delete -> ~IMPFramesource ->
+  // destroy() -> IMP_FrameSource_DestroyChn frees the pool; next CreateChn is clean.
+  if (global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder) {
+    global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder->deinit();
+    delete global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder;
+    global_video[encChn]->imp_encoder = nullptr;
+  }
+
+  if (global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource) {
+    global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource->disable();
+    delete global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource;
+    global_video[encChn]->imp_framesource = nullptr;
+  }

   {
     std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(global_video[encChn]->privacy_mutex);
